; E168 Phase 12, G4 -- E161 G0 re-expressed on the floor. THE RANK FLOOR.
;
; The historical defect, in docs/testing-floors.md:197-202's own words: making
; BOTH providers emit the same wrong marker left the provider-vs-provider
; differential reporting `ok`. "Only the gate's own expected blob -- a rank-2
; source OUTSIDE BOTH LEGS -- caught it." The operative phrase is outside both
; legs. (The page said "the fixed committed blob" until 2026-08-25; the anchor
; was the heredoc pin inside test-module-kind.sh, not the gitignored compiler
; blob -- testing-floors.md:216-262.)
; The two legs were genuinely distinct, so `df-common-mode` cannot see this;
; what was missing is that EVERY expectation came from inside the comparison.
;
; Mechanized: `differ` mints its `Expect` at the WEAKER leg's provenance, so two
; in-tree providers mint `pv-derived` (rank 4), and `gate-verdict`'s clause 2
; scores the gate `g-inadequate` unless `checks` ALSO carries an expectation at
; rank <= 3 -- one not derived from the thing under test. Rank <= 3 and not <= 2
; because :163-164 states the requirement as independence from the thing under
; test, which ranks 1, 2 and 3 all satisfy.
;
; Three cases, one exit code (a fixture with more than one internal case still
; counts once -- inflating the assertion count is how an unsourced figure gets
; built). It CROSSES, building two programs through one cap, but reads no file,
; so its answer does not depend on the working directory.
;
; exit 0 = all three; 1 = the differential minted nothing to judge;
; 2 = differential-only scored GREEN (the 2026 defect verbatim); 3 = it scored
; red for the wrong reason; 4/5 = the rank-2 fix did not clear the floor;
; 6/7 = the rank-3 anchor did not clear it, i.e. the boundary moved.

(import "evidence/test-floor")

; both legs are handed the SAME source and both run it, so they agree -- and
; they agree on 41, which is wrong. Two providers wrong the same way.
(def wrong-src Bytes (str->bytes "(def main (-> I64 I64) (lam (n) 41))"))

; adequacy is not what is under test here, so it is a mutant that WAS run and
; WAS killed; clause 1 must not be what reddens these gates.
(def killed MutRun (mu-killed "a mutant that ran and died" (ob-exit 42) (ob-exit 41)))

; rank 2 -- the SHAPE of the historical fix: a fixed artifact outside both legs.
; Its key is `obk-given`: nothing here rebuilds it, the harness supplies it.
;
; ⚑ THE TWO FIELDS ARE REAL AS OF 2026-08-25 (E170 W0.4), and the history is kept
; because it is the point. This fixture judges the RANK ARITHMETIC -- that a
; rank-2 anchor clears a floor a rank-4 differential-minted expectation cannot --
; and `gate-verdict` ranks the CONSTRUCTOR: it does not open the path or check the
; digest, so any two strings buy rank 2 here. That is still true, and it is why
; the fields were once written as `scaffold/tests/golden/e161-marker.blob` and
; `"sha256:committed"` -- a file that never existed and a digest nobody computed,
; "a fixed COMMITTED artifact" doing rhetorical work no artifact backed. Replacing
; those strings with placeholders was the honest move at the time, and this
; comment then recorded the two conditions for closing it properly: a floor that
; OPENS the named path and compares the digest, and something that checks a claim
; nothing opens.
;
; BOTH now exist, so the placeholders go. `oracle-ask`'s `or-fixed` arm compares
; its digest (W0.3), and `ledger-lint` check S (W0.4) asserts clauses 1-3 of the
; admission test -- tracked, not gitignored, digest non-empty -- for every anchor
; path written down in scaffold/**/*.chiral, which is the only mechanical check a
; `pv-fixed` can have: it is an `ExProv`, and nothing in chirality opens one. The
; anchor below is a real 24-byte committed file whose bytes ARE its digest
; (testing-floors.md:250-252), and it is not minted to justify this sentence: it
; holds the exact marker BOTH module providers emit after `prelude`, which is the
; E161 G0 anchor class -- the gate that reported ok with both legs emitting the
; same wrong marker.
(def fixed-check Expect
  (ex (obk-given "a fixed artifact outside both legs: the committed end-module marker")
      (ob-exit 41)
      (pv-fixed "scaffold/tests/fixed/end-module-prelude.txt"
                (str->bytes "\n(end-module \"prelude\")\n"))))

; rank 3 -- an anchor written down from what the form MEANS. It must clear the
; floor too, which is what makes <= 3 a chosen boundary rather than an accident.
(def meaning-check Expect
  (ex (obk-given "what the program means")
      (ob-exit 41)
      (pv-meaning "the source returns 41, so the process exits 41")))

(def one-obs (List Obs) (cons (ob-exit 41) nil))
(def two-obs (List Obs) (cons (ob-exit 41) (cons (ob-exit 41) nil)))

(def judge (-> DiffExpect I64)
  (lam (minted)
    ; case 1: EVERY expectation came from inside the comparison.
    (case (gate-verdict (gate "markers agree" (the (List Expect) nil) (cons minted nil) killed) one-obs)
      ((g-green nm c w) 2)
      ((g-red nm why)   3)
      ((g-inadequate nm lb)
        ; case 2: the historical fix -- add the fixed expected blob (rank 2).
        (case (gate-verdict (gate "markers agree" (cons fixed-check nil) (cons minted nil) killed) two-obs)
          ((g-red nm2 why2)      4)
          ((g-inadequate nm2 l2) 5)
          ((g-green nm2 c2 w2)
            ; case 3: a rank-3 anchor clears it too. Without this case, raising
            ; the floor to <= 2 would leave the rank-2 anchor green and the
            ; mutant that moves the boundary could not fire at all.
            (case (gate-verdict (gate "markers agree" (cons meaning-check nil) (cons minted nil) killed) two-obs)
              ((g-red nm3 why3)      6)
              ((g-inadequate nm3 l3) 7)
              ((g-green nm3 c3 w3)   0))))))))

(def compile-main (=> I64 I64)
  (lam (n)
    (case (differ (builder "B1")
                  (oracle (or-native "the fold provider"))
                  (oracle (or-native "the emit provider"))
                  wrong-src)
      ((differ-r d bld2)
        (case bld2
          ((builder v)
            (case (diff-expect (obk-given "both providers' marker") d)
              ((ex-refused why) 1)
              ((ex-ok minted)   (judge minted)))))))))
